@page "/exboard"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Board

<DocsPage Product="ExBoard" Title="Overview"
          Lead="A Board is JSON an AI composes once and a workflow runs again and again without AI: grids, pivots and charts laid out in rows over typed Board Inputs, each input checked against its data's shape before anything is drawn.">

    <DocsSection Title="Composed once, run without AI">
        <p>
            The data a workflow obtains is not known until it runs: which columns, which types, a grid, a pivot or a
            chart. A Board says it in JSON. Its <code>inputs</code> name the data and declare each column's type, written
            while whoever composed it knew what the data means; its <code>views</code> are grids, pivots and charts over
            those inputs; its <code>layout</code> is rows of views, each its share of the row, never free placement. The
            Board is saved, and each run binds every input to that run's data. ExBoard holds no workflow: it reads the
            Board, checks its inputs, and draws it.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>BoardSpec.Read</code> reads a Board, and refuses what cannot be drawn with one sentence naming the path
            and the reason, as a Chart Spec does: a version other than 1, a member a Board does not have, a view naming
            a column its input does not declare, a decimal column with no format, a layout that leaves a view out or
            places one twice. A grid view is <a href="exgrid/columns">a grid built at run time</a>, a pivot view holds a
            Pivot Layout, and a chart view is a Chart Spec whose series name inputs in their <code>data</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <code>ExBoard</code> component draws a read Board with each input bound to a Snapshot. Binding checks the
            Snapshot's shape against the input's declaration first; a column missing or of another type draws nothing but
            the refusal, naming the input and the column, so a change upstream is caught on the run that meets it. Grids
            and pivots stretch to their share of the row; a chart is drawn at its share of the Board's <code>Width</code>.
            Change the Board below and draw again: remove the grid's <code>format</code>, have the pivot place a field
            the input does not declare, or make the chart's series an <code>amountPerPeriod</code>, which a day's
            P&amp;L with no interval cannot be drawn as.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(DeskBoardBuiltin)" Mud="typeof(DeskBoardMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Board/DeskBoard.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Links narrow by values">
        <p>
            A link names a grid or a pivot, the views it narrows and the columns it compares:
            <code>{"from": "blotter", "to": ["byTrader", "daily"], "on": ["desk"]}</code>. Select rows in the grid, in any
            column, and each view the link reaches shows only the rows whose values in those columns are the selected
            rows' values. In the Board above, select an FX trade: the pivot shows FX's traders and the chart FX's days.
            A selection of several rows, with Ctrl or Shift, lets through every one of their values.
        </p>
        <p>
            It narrows by values, not by rows, so it reaches views over other inputs that declare the same columns, and
            keeps its meaning when the data moves on. On two columns it lets through the selected rows' own pairs:
            rows (Rates, Jan) and (Credit, Feb) let through no (Rates, Feb). Text is compared as a grid's value list
            compares it, without regard to case. A narrowed grid keeps the sort and the filter set on it; a pivot is
            computed again over the rows let through, and a chart drawn from them. When the selection is dropped,
            because the grid was sorted or filtered or the selection cleared, the narrowing goes with it: a view is
            never left narrowed by a selection that is no longer there.
        </p>
        <p>
            A link from a pivot narrows by the cells selected in its report. A cell stands for the rows under it: a
            trader's cell for that trader's trades, a subtotal for every Item below it, a grand total for every row the
            pivot shows. An Item the pivot hides is under no cell. In the Board above, the pivot links to the chart on
            <code>trader</code>: select Chen's cell and the chart shows Chen's day alone. A pivot's link compares only
            fields placed in its rows or columns, since those are what a cell names. When another link narrows the
            pivot, its report is computed again, its selection is dropped, and what its cells narrowed is let go. A
            selection that reaches rows the report has not drawn yet narrows nothing, and the Board says so under
            the pivot rather than narrowing by part of it.
        </p>
        <p>
            Reading refuses a link that starts at a chart, compares a field a pivot does not place in its rows or
            columns, narrows its own view, names a view or a column that is not there, compares a column of one type with one of another, or
            would close a loop: links run one way.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Outputs raised on a confirm">
        <p>
            What the user chooses is handed on to the workflow's next step only when they say so. An output names a
            grid, the columns whose values it hands on and the label of the action that raises it:
            <code>"outputs": {"review": {"from": "blotter", "on": ["day", "trader"], "confirm": "Send to review"}}</code>.
            The action is drawn under the grid; a selection on the way raises nothing, and a press with nothing
            selected says so. Select rows in the Board above and press <em>Send to review</em>: <code>OnOutput</code> is raised
            with a <code>BoardOutput</code>, shown here as its JSON.
        </p>
        <p>
            An output is a value condition, the selected rows' values in its columns, with the version of the input's
            data they were taken against. It hands on values, not rows: every row holding the chosen values is taken,
            so an output meant to name single rows names a column that tells them apart, such as a trade's id. The next
            step runs it where the data lives, or takes the rows with <code>BoardOutput.Take</code>, which refuses data
            of another version, and other data at version 0, which names none: the values the user chose are taken
            from the data they chose them in, or nothing is.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What the user changes, kept as a Board Diff">
        <p>
            Running a Board never rewrites it. The user may still sort and filter a grid, drag its columns' widths
            and order, pin or hide a column, rearrange a pivot, hide a chart's series, narrow its range or switch its
            axis. Each change raises <code>OnDiff</code> with the whole <code>BoardDiff</code>: one part for each view
            that differs from what the Board writes, a grid's View State and the columns hidden from it, a pivot's
            layout, a chart's series, range and axis. A selection, and the narrowing a link makes of it, is not a
            change to the Board and raises nothing. Sort the blotter above to see its JSON.
        </p>
        <p>
            The host keeps <code>ToJson()</code> for its user and hands it back next time: it reads it with
            <code>BoardDiff.Read</code> and passes it as <code>Diff</code>, laid against the Board as it is then. A part that no longer fits, a
            view the Board no longer has, a column its grid no longer shows, a filter on a column its input now declares
            another kind, is dropped by name and the rest applies;
            the Board says what it dropped above its views. A column the Board has added since is shown, not taken for
            one the user hid. Press <em>Draw with my changes</em>, or first remove the blotter's P&amp;L column from the
            JSON, to see both. Making the user's view the default is writing a new Board.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The draw_board tool">
        <p>
            <code>BoardSpec.Tool</code> is the tool definition a host hands its model, in the Chart Spec's form: a name,
            <code>draw_board</code>, a description and the JSON Schema of its input. The input is the Board and the Data
            Handle each input is bound to now: <code>{"board": {…}, "data": {"trades": "h-7f3a"}}</code>. The model never
            sees a row. What it is told of a capability's table is its shape, written by
            <code>BoardSpec.DeclarationOf(shape)</code> as the declaration a Board writes, so the types it declares are
            the source's, not guessed from JSON.
        </p>
        <p>
            When the model calls it, the host passes the input and its lookup from a handle to its Snapshot to
            <code>BoardSpec.Answer</code> and returns <code>Text</code>. An accepted Board is said in words, view by view in
            the layout's order, a chart by its summary, then its links and outputs, so the model can check it against what
            it meant. A refused one is one sentence naming the path, such as
            <code>data.trades: "h-9" is not known: name a Data Handle a capability returned.</code> The answer also carries
            the Board, which is what a workflow saves and runs again without the model, and the Board bound to draw now.
            Edit the call below and leave the field.
        </p>
        <p>
            In TypeScript the tool is <code>boardSpecTool</code>, the shape is said by <code>boardDeclarationOf(shape)</code>,
            and <code>answerBoardSpec(call, handle =&gt; snapshot)</code> answers with <code>accepted</code>,
            <code>text</code>, <code>board</code> and <code>bound</code>, in the same words as the C# tool, so a host on
            Node answers a model as a .NET host does.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(BoardToolCallBuiltin)" Mud="typeof(BoardToolCallMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Board/BoardToolCall.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A Chart Spec is a Board of one view">
        <p>
            A Board's chart view is a <a href="exchart/spec">Chart Spec</a> whose series name the Board's inputs. It is
            read against the inputs' declarations when the Board is read, so a series on a column its input does not
            declare is refused then, by path, with the rest of the Board, not when data first arrives.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>draw_chart</code> keeps naming Data Handles while an AI composes. <code>BoardSpec.OfChart(spec, shapes)</code>
            makes that spec a Board of one view, <code>BoardSpec.ChartViewId</code>: each handle the spec names becomes an
            input declared with the shape of the table the host holds for it, and a refusal keeps the spec's own path, as
            the tool says it. Bind it with each handle's Snapshot under the handle's name. Below, the spec is a
            <code>draw_chart</code> call's; write <code>"y": "book"</code> and leave the field to see it refused. In
            TypeScript it is <code>boardOfChart(spec, handle =&gt; shape)</code>, and the view's id
            <code>boardChartViewId</code>.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ChartSpecBoardBuiltin)" Mud="typeof(ChartSpecBoardMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Board/ChartSpecBoard.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A Board over a server">
        <p>
            An input too large to download is bound to a <code>BoardSource</code> in <code>Sources</code>, beside the
            Snapshots in <code>Data</code>. A <code>BoardSource</code> is your own code: it answers the input's shape, which
            is checked against the declaration before anything is drawn; fetches a grid's rows a page at a time, as a
            Snapshot; and, if it can, makes a pivot's <code>PivotSource</code> (<code>Pivot</code>), such as a
            <code>FetchingPivotSource</code> whose server answers Leaf Aggregates, the pivot building its report and totals
            from them. Every fetch and every Pivot Source is handed the link conditions narrowing the view (<code>BoardQuery.Narrowing</code>), for the server to execute where the
            data lives. When a selection changes them, the view is asked again. The Board JSON is the same either way:
            nothing in it says where an input is held.
        </p>
        <p>
            A selection is read from the rows fetched. One that reaches rows the grid has not fetched, a Ctrl+A over
            twenty thousand trades, narrows nothing and hands nothing on, and the Board says so under the grid rather
            than read part of it. An output hands on the version of the page its rows were read from.
        </p>
        <p>
            A chart over such an input asks the source's <code>Thin</code> for its points: a column across, a column of
            values, a range and 2,048 pixels (<code>BoundBoard.ChartPixels</code>), answered as
            <code>ChartSource.Thin</code> answers, each pixel keeping its first, last, lowest and highest row. Only those
            cross to the browser, and the tooltip still counts every row under a pixel. The chart is asked again when a
            link narrows it and when you narrow its range, which is then drawn at full detail; an answer a later ask
            has replaced is dropped. It saves no CSV and shows no table, which would be the points kept dressed as the
            data. A series it cannot thin, an observation's points or a colour per point, is refused by path.
        </p>
        <p>
            A link from a pivot over such an input asks the source's <code>Under</code> for the values under the cells
            selected, since the rows are on the server: a subtotal or the grand total stands for every Item below it.
            <code>BoardSource.ValuesUnder</code> is the reference a server answers as. The views the link reaches keep
            their rows until the answer comes. A source with no <code>Thin</code> under a chart, or no
            <code>Under</code> under such a link, is refused at binding. Select FX in the pivot below and the chart is
            FX's; select the Grand Total and it is every desk's again.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ServerBoardBuiltin)" Mud="typeof(ServerBoardMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Board/ServerBoard.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="From TypeScript">
        <p>
            <code>@@ex-grid/board</code> is ExBoard in TypeScript, the package ExBoard's React and Blazor packages move onto
            (ADR-0370), built in this repository and published with 0.3.0. <code>readBoard</code> reads a Board and refuses
            what <code>BoardSpec.Read</code> refuses, at the same path and in the same words: both readers run the same
            cases, under <code>tests/cases/board/</code>. <code>bindBoard</code> binds it to Snapshots from
            <code>@@ex-grid/data</code>: each grid view's columns are built from its input's shape, a grid's or a pivot's
            selection narrows the views its links reach, and an output is raised only by <code>confirm</code>, which
            answers nothing while nothing is chosen. Binding also refuses, naming the path, what a grid over a Snapshot
            cannot do: a sort by a column the grid does not show, and a filter that compares a column with a value of
            another type. <code>BoardDiff</code> reads, writes and fits a Board Diff as ExBoard does, to the byte, and
            <code>bindBoard(board, data, { diff })</code> lays one over the Board, naming in <code>dropped</code> what no
            longer fits; <code>bound.diff</code> is what the user has changed since.
        </p>
        <p>
            A Board over a server binds in TypeScript too. Hand <code>createBoard</code> a <code>sources</code> record beside
            <code>data</code>, or call <code>bindBoardThrough(board, data, sources)</code>. A source is an object of your
            own. Its <code>shape</code> answers the input's shape, which is checked before anything is drawn, while the
            Board says it is asking. Its <code>fetch</code> answers a grid's query with a <code>ShapedPage</code>. Its
            <code>pivot(narrowing)</code> makes the pivot's source, a <code>FetchingPivotSource</code> answering Leaf
            Aggregates from your server, or any other <code>PivotSource</code>. Its <code>thin</code> answers a chart's
            points, as <code>thinChartData</code> thins, at <code>boardChartPixels</code> (2,048). Its <code>under</code>
            answers the values under a pivot's cells, as <code>valuesUnder</code> reads them. Every question carries the
            link conditions narrowing the view, and the behaviour is the C# Board's, held by the same tests: a
            selection past the rows fetched says so under the grid, and an answer a later ask replaced is dropped. One
            thing more is said in TypeScript: a fetched grid whose source fails says so under the grid.
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>createBoard(element, {
    board, data: {}, temporal: Temporal,
    sources: {
        trades: {
            shape: () => api.shape('trades'),                                   // checked before anything is drawn
            fetch: (query, signal) => api.page('trades', query, signal),          // { rows: Snapshot, start, totalCount }
            pivot: narrowing => new FetchingPivotSource(fields, features, api.pivotFetchers('trades', narrowing)),
            thin: (query, signal) => api.thin('trades', query, signal),           // as thinChartData thins
            under: (query, signal) => api.under('trades', query, signal),         // as valuesUnder reads
        },
    },
});</code></pre>
        <p>
            <code>createBoard(element, options)</code> draws a bound Board into an element, as the <code>ExBoard</code>
            component does: its grids with <code>createGrid</code>, its pivots with <code>createPivot</code> and its
            charts with <code>createChart</code>, laid out in its rows, with each output's confirm button under its grid.
            A Board that does not bind draws only its refusal. It raises <code>onOutput</code> on a confirm and
            <code>onDiff</code> with the whole Board Diff on each change the user makes, and takes the Diff back as
            <code>diff</code>. A grid showing a date column needs <code>temporal</code>, the Temporal namespace its
            filter makes dates with. <code>@@ex-grid/board-react</code> is the React component, <code>ExBoard</code>,
            whose props are those options; given no <code>locale</code>, it takes the browser's first language a pivot
            writes in.
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>import { BoardDiff, createBoard } from '@@ex-grid/board';

const handle = createBoard(element, {
    board, data: { trades }, width: 1000, locale: 'en-US', temporal: Temporal,
    diff: saved === null ? undefined : BoardDiff.read(saved),   // what the user changed last time
    onDiff: diff => localStorage.setItem('board', diff.toJson()),
    onOutput: output => send(output.toJson()),
});</code></pre>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>import { bindBoard, boardInput, readBoard } from '@@ex-grid/board';

const board = readBoard(json);                                  // throws a BoardRefusedError naming the path
const input = boardInput(board, 'trades');                      // undefined for an input the Board does not declare
if (input === undefined) throw new Error("The Board declares no input 'trades'.");
const bound = bindBoard(board, { trades: input.fromJson(rowsJson) });  // or a Snapshot read from Arrow
bound.select('blotter', selection);                             // a grid's selection narrows its links' views
const output = bound.confirm('chosen');                         // the values chosen with their version, or undefined while none are</code></pre>
        <p>
            Read this site for React and every Example on this page runs on <code>@@ex-grid/board-react</code>, with its
            code under <em>Show code</em>: the server's Example answers its source in a few lines of TypeScript over rows
            in memory, ordering a page with <code>GridSource.from</code>, as a server's query is written to match.
        </p>
        <p>
            React's ExBoard has no Wrapper either. Its <code>chrome</code> takes each kind of view's Chrome, as that
            product's component takes it: <code>grid</code> as ExGrid's, <code>pivot</code> as ExPivot's and
            <code>chart</code> as ExChart's. The grid's also dresses the pivots' report grids where <code>pivot</code> names
            no grid of its own, as <code>Chrome</code> does on the Board above. So a Mantine application hands it
            <code>mantineChrome</code>, <code>mantinePivotChrome</code> and <code>mantineChartChrome</code>, inside an
            element of the Wrappers' classes, <code>mantine-ex-grid mantine-ex-pivot mantine-ex-chart</code>, which map the
            views' colours onto the theme; an MUI application does the same with MUI's. Read this site for React and pick
            Mantine or MUI: the first Example on this page runs so. The Script Core takes the same object as
            <code>BoardOptions.chrome</code>, with mount functions in place of render functions.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A Board drawn in the browser">
        <p>
            Blazor's ExBoard runs on the same TypeScript core as React's, as ExGrid and ExPivot do: every Example on
            this site draws <code>ExBoard.Script.ExBoard</code>. Binding,
            layout, links, the confirm and the Board Diff run in the browser on both hosting models, so on Blazor Server
            a selection, a narrowing or a sort never crosses the circuit. It takes a <code>BoardReading</code>,
            <code>Data</code>, <code>Width</code>, <code>Culture</code>, <code>OnOutput</code>, <code>Diff</code> and
            <code>OnDiff</code>. The Board crosses as its JSON. Each Snapshot crosses once, as the Arrow stream
            <code>ExGrid.Data.Arrow</code> writes; a new <code>Data</code> holding the same Snapshot sends nothing again. An
            output comes back to .NET read against the very Snapshot it was taken from, so <code>Take</code> holds it to
            that data's version, and a Diff the Board raised and you hand back binds nothing again. The script it runs is
            bundled into the package, at an address that changes with its content.
        </p>
        <p>
            It takes <code>Chrome</code>, <code>PivotChrome</code> and <code>ChartChrome</code> as the Board above does:
            the grid Chrome dresses the grids and the pivots' report grids, the pivot Chrome the pivots, its grid Chrome
            the report grids where no <code>Chrome</code> is given, and the chart Chrome the charts. Each part a Chrome
            names in its <code>ScriptSlots</code> is drawn by its Razor inside the view the browser draws, as on
            <code>ExGrid.Script.ExGrid</code>, <code>ExPivot.Script.ExPivot</code> and <code>ExChart.Script.ExChart</code>,
            so the MudBlazor switch below dresses the same Board with <code>MudGridChrome</code>,
            <code>MudPivotChrome</code> and <code>MudChartChrome</code>. Which parts are filled is read as the Board
            attaches: Chromes naming other slots need a new ExBoard.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreBoard)" Mud="typeof(ScriptCoreBoardMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Board/DeskBoard.cs"])" />
        <p>
            It takes <code>Sources</code> too, the same <code>BoardSource</code> objects as the Board above. Each stays in
            .NET: the browser asks it through .NET for the input's shape, for each page a grid fetches with its query and
            the links' narrowing, for a chart's thinned points and for the values under a pivot's selected cells, and a
            question the Board gives up is cancelled through the token your source is handed. A pivot reads the
            <code>PivotSource</code> your <code>Pivot</code> makes over each narrowing through .NET too, as
            <code>ExPivot.Script.ExPivot</code> reads one: the engine in the browser builds the report and its totals from
            the Leaf Aggregates it answers. A page crosses back as its Arrow stream and a chart's points and the values
            under cells with every value exact. An output taken from rows the server held comes back with the version the
            server answered them at, and <code>Take</code> holds it to that version.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreServerBoard)" Files="@(["Examples/Board/ServerBoard.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The package">
        <p>
            ExBoard is one package, <code>ExBoard</code>, published at the family's one version with exact references
            to the ExGrid, ExPivot and ExChart it was built with, and to <code>ExGrid.Data.Arrow</code>, in which
            <code>ExBoard.Script.ExBoard</code> sends each Snapshot to the browser. It has no MudBlazor Wrapper: each view already takes
            its product's Chrome, and the Board passes <code>Chrome</code>, <code>PivotChrome</code> and
            <code>ChartChrome</code> through, so a MudBlazor application hands it the Wrappers' Chromes, as the
            Examples above do under the MudBlazor switch.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

